    "vignette": "A 26‐year‐old woman presented with a 1‐year history of pruritic plaques on her lower extremities that did not improve with topical corticosteroids. On examination, there were well‐defined hyperpigmented, hyperkeratotic plaques with thick adherent scales and a peripheral rim of erythema distributed symmetrically over the dorsum of both feet and around the knees. Laboratory studies showed a normal complete blood count, negative hepatitis C and B serologies, and normal levels of aspartate and alanine aminotransferases. Plasma zinc was at the lower end of normal (72 μg/dL; reference range 70–120 μg/dL), and thyroid‐stimulating hormone was elevated at 16.6 mIU/L (reference range 0.5–5 mIU/L). A 4‐mm punch biopsy of a lesion revealed hyperkeratosis, parakeratosis, focal subcorneal clefting with keratinocyte necrosis, slight intracellular edema, and a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Necrolytic acral erythema\nB. Psoriasis was considered because of hyperkeratotic plaques and prior steroid treatment, but it was unlikely given the\nC. Lichenoid lesions were in the differential based on the appearance of scaly plaques\nD. Atopic dermatitis was also considered given the pruritic nature of the plaques\nE. Necrolytic migratory erythema was considered because of necrolytic histologic changes, but glucagonoma was unlikely in\nF. Necrolytic acral erythema was diagnosed on the basis of characteristic histopathology combined with zinc deficiency and\nG. None\nH. None\n",
